gher self-control","a":["Eddie M. W. Tong","Kenny W. T. Tan","Agapera A.B. Chor","Emmeline P.S. Koh","Jehanne S.Y. Lee","Regina W. Y. Tan"],"d":"2015-09-26","abs":"Prior evidence and existing theories imply that humility engenders intra- and inter-personal attributes that facilitate self-regulatory abilities. Four experiments were conducted to test the hypothesis that humility predicts enhanced self-control. Participants who recalled humility experiences were found to be better able at sustaining their physical stamina in a handgrip task (Studies 1 and 4), resisting indulgence in chocolates (Study 2), and persevering in a frustrating tracing task (Study 3) than those who recalled neutral experiences. Studies 3 and 4 demonstrated that the effect of humility was distinct from that of self-esteem, which did not affect self-control. Study 2 ruled out two alternative hypotheses concerning achievement and compliance motives. We discuss how the findings might relate to outcomes associated with humility as evidenced in past studies.","oa":"hybrid","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jesp.2015.09.008","pdf":"","k":["Humility","Psychology","Social psychology","Self-control","Compliance (psychology)","Affect (linguistics)","Control (management)","Theology"]},{"doi":"10.1016/j.jesp.2015.09.006","t":"The empirical benefits of conceptual rigor: Systematic articulation of conceptual hypotheses can reduce the risk of non-replicable results (and facilitate novel discoveries too)","a":["Mark Schaller"],"d":"2015-09-24","abs":"","oa":"closed","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jesp.2015.09.006","pdf":"","k":["Conceptualization","Normative","Psychology","Conceptual framework","Context (archaeology)","Empirical research","Rigour","Replication (statistics)"]},{"doi":"10.1016/j.jesp.2015.09.004","t":"An advantage of appearing mean or lazy: Amplified impressions of competence or warmth after mixed descriptions","a":["Nicolas Kervyn","Hilary B. Bergsieker","Fiona Grignard","Vincent Yzerbyt"],"d"